1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Leena: Welcome to Workbench. My guest Omar studies how teams share information. Today we are discussing focus and meeting habits.

2
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Omar: My claim is that frequent context switching makes careful work harder. That is my interpretation of the teams I have observed, not a measured universal percentage.

3
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Leena: For listeners, try a distraction-free work block this week and note what interrupted it.

4
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Omar: Notifications are only one source of interruption. Unclear priorities can force people to switch tasks even when their devices are quiet.

5
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Leena: Read the team's meeting agenda before joining the call. That is my second recommendation to listeners.

6
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Omar: A useful agenda tells people what kind of contribution is needed. A discussion and a decision require different preparation. I do not have a single duration that works for every meeting.

7
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Leena: Next episode, we will return to this topic with a discussion about asynchronous status updates.

8
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:10,000
Omar: Written context can help colleagues in different time zones, provided they can find it and ask questions.

9
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Leena: Thanks for listening to Workbench.
